on, not a shell-quoted variable, same treatment as --dangerously-skip-permissions on the same line - no shell_quote needed.\n\nThis flag suppresses every global Claude Code hook (both plugin-registered and hand-written in ~/.claude/settings.json), which is the actual mechanism that caused the incident. It deliberately does NOT touch: OAuth/keychain auth, the project-local turn-end supervision hook this same script writes into each worktree's .claude/settings.local.json (the Stop/StopFailure/SessionEnd/UserPromptSubmit hooks bin/fm-crew-state.sh's busy-state tracking depends on), project-level AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md/.claude/skills, or any MCP server crew tasks need. Two alternative flags (--bare, --safe-mode) were investigated and rejected: --bare breaks OAuth/keychain auth entirely, and --safe-mode also kills the project-local turn-end hook with no working escape hatch - only --setting-sources project,local satisfies all constraints.\n\nTask-specific verification performed (beyond the investigation scope, which was investigation-only):\n1. Re-grepped bin/fm-spawn.sh and all of bin/ for any other claude launch-construction path beyond launch_template() - confirmed none exists; the relaunch path also routes through the same function.\n2. Confirmed launch_template()'s claude) case has no branching on the parameter (ship/scout/secondmate all share it), so the fix applies uniformly with no special-casing needed.\n3. Live-tested for real (not just read the diff): spawned an actual claude-harness scout crewmate into a disposable scratch worktree using the fixed code, and confirmed (a) it authenticated and started normally via OAuth, (b) bin/fm-crew-state.sh correctly reported working/busy state during an active turn and the busy-state file correctly transitioned busy->idle across a full UserPromptSubmit->Stop hook cycle, matching the exact turn-end mechanism the watcher depends on, and (c) the spawned scout's transcript was completely free of the global pressure text and unrelated skill-invocation injection that this very session was hit with before the fix (no 'MAGIC KEYWORDS DETECTED', no 'boulder never stops' pressure line, no ultrawork-restore banner) - it correctly still answered YES to a literal probe question about whether its context mentions oh-my-claudecode/CONDUCTOR/ultrawork, which is the expected and documented residual: the flag suppresses hooks, not the static global CLAUDE.md/rules/*.md prose text itself.\n\nSecond deliverable: added a dated documentation note to the harness-adapters skill (.agents/skills/harness-adapters/SKILL.md), under the existing claude section, explaining this residual risk - the global CLAUDE.md/rules/*.md prose still loads as inert instruction text even after this fix (hooks are suppressed, not the manifesto text), so a future firstmate session that sees a crewmate report unexplained 'continue working' pressure or an unrelated skill-invocation demand not traceable to its own brief should recognize this as very likely this known leak rather than treating it as a new prompt-injection security incident, while still respecting a crewmate's instinct to pause and check first. This is documentation only, not a technical fix for the residual (no clean flag exists that removes it without breaking auth or the turn-end hook, per the investigation).\n\nExplicit exclusion: nothing else about crew/secondmate launch behavior was changed. The existing CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR forwarding logic elsewhere in fm-spawn.sh (a different, unrelated mechanism for firstmate's own store-forwarding) was deliberately left untouched.\n-----END USER INTENT-----\n\n## Test-quality rule\n\nNever add a test whose only evidence is that it opens, reads, greps, parses, or\nsnapshots implementation source code and finds or omits particular strings,\ntokens, lines, commands, function names, prompt phrases, regex matches, AST\nshapes, or incidental snapshots. That does not prove behavior: matching text\ncan be dead or commented out, and a behavior-preserving refactor can change it.\n\nInstead execute a public or executable interface and assert observable behavior,\nstate, output, side effects, and failure modes. For machine-consumed declarative\nartifacts such as workflow YAML, JSON, policy, .gitignore, or generated\nconfiguration, invoke the real consumer when feasible or parse into a typed or\nnormalized semantic model and assert meaning. A raw substring or regex over the\nfile is still the anti-pattern.\n\nReading a file is legitimate when the file is itself generated public output, a\nserialized protocol, persisted state, an intentional snapshot, or another\nexplicitly owned text or byte contract. Name that contract, and do not use its\ncontents as a proxy that unrelated code works. A natural-language prompt or\ninstruction is not proven effective because its source contains a sentence.\nDeterministic CI may test the final emitted prompt delivered to an agent as an\nintentional generated interface; model interpretation belongs in\ndevelopment-only evaluation, not live-LLM CI.\n\nFor a regression, reproduce the reported failure when feasible: the test should\nfail before the fix and pass after it.\n\n\nContinue working in ultrawork mode until all tasks are complete.\n\n</session-restore>\n\n---\n"}}
